  },
  {
    question: 'When did California Prop 19 take effect?',
    answer:
      'California Proposition 19 took effect on February 16, 2021 for parent-to-child and grandparent-to-grandchild transfers. It supersedes the prior Proposition 58 rules (Cal. Rev. & Tax. Code § 63.1) for all transfers completed on or after that date. Transfers completed before February 16, 2021 remain governed by the old rules.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do both spouses need to sign the quitclaim deed if the property is only in one parent\'s name?',
    answer:
      "If the property is titled only in one parent's name and is not their community property, only that parent needs to sign. However, if the property is community property of a married couple — even if titled only in one spouse's name — both spouses should sign to convey the full community interest. California is a community property state (Cal. Fam. Code § 750), meaning property acquired during a marriage is generally presumed to be community property. If there is any uncertainty, both spouses should sign and consult an attorney.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I use a quitclaim deed or a grant deed for a parent-to-child transfer in California?',
    answer:
      "Both are legally effective for transferring title. A quitclaim deed transfers whatever interest the grantor holds — no more, no less. A grant deed (the more common choice in California arm's-length sales) includes two implied warranties: that the grantor has not already conveyed the property to someone else, and that the property is free of encumbrances made by the grantor. For a parent-to-child transfer where the parties trust each other and the title history is known, a quitclaim deed is commonly used and is legally sufficient.",
